{"id":"ALG-E07","workedExamples":[{"id":"ALG-E07-we-01","title":"Solve a/b = c/x by cross products: 6/9 = 8/x","steps":[{"prompt":"You have the proportion 6/9 = 8/x — two equal ratios. What does cross-multiplying mean, and which terms pair up?","reveal":"Cross products means multiply each numerator by the OTHER fraction's denominator. The correct diagonals are 6 with x, and 9 with 8. So 6 · x = 9 · 8. The trap is pairing 6 with 9 (same fraction) — those are not a cross product."},{"prompt":"Write the cross-product equation, then simplify the all-numbers side.","reveal":"6 · x = 9 · 8, which is 6x = 72. We multiplied the two known numbers 9 and 8 to get 72."},{"prompt":"Solve 6x = 72. What undoes multiplying by 6?","reveal":"Divide both sides by 6: x = 72 / 6 = 12. So x = 12."},{"prompt":"Check: does 6/9 = 8/12?","reveal":"Both reduce to 2/3 (6/9 = 2/3 and 8/12 = 2/3). The ratios match, so x = 12 is correct."}]},{"id":"ALG-E07-we-02","title":"Set up a proportion with consistent units: 3 books cost $12, find the cost of 5 books","steps":[{"prompt":"3 books cost $12; we want the cost of 5 books. Before solving, decide what goes on top and what goes on the bottom of each ratio.","reveal":"Pick a rule and keep it for BOTH ratios. Use books on top and dollars on the bottom: books/dollars. The common error is writing books/dollars on one side and dollars/books on the other — mismatched units give a wrong answer."},{"prompt":"Fill in both ratios using books/dollars, with x as the unknown cost.","reveal":"3/12 = 5/x. Left: 3 books over $12. Right: 5 books over the unknown cost x. Top is books and bottom is dollars on BOTH sides."},{"prompt":"Cross-multiply along the correct diagonals and solve.","reveal":"3 · x = 12 · 5, so 3x = 60, and x = 60 / 3 = 20."},{"prompt":"State the answer with its unit and sanity-check it.","reveal":"x = $20 for 5 books. That is more than $12 for 3 books, which makes sense — more books cost more. Flipping one ratio would have given a smaller, nonsensical number."}]}],"problems":{"p1":[{"id":"ALG-E07-p1-baseball-01","version":1,"skillId":"ALG-E07","phase":1,"sport":"baseball","prompt":"A hitter has 27 hits in 90 at-bats. At that exact rate, how many hits would he have in 150 at-bats?","visual":"table","answer":{"kind":"numeric","value":"45"},"misconceptionMap":{"50":"cross-multiplies-wrong-pairs"},"hints":["Set up hits/at-bats = hits/at-bats: 27/90 = x/150.","27/90 reduces to 0.3 hits per at-bat. Multiply by 150."],"difficulty":1},{"id":"ALG-E07-p1-baseball-02","version":1,"skillId":"ALG-E07","phase":1,"sport":"baseball","prompt":"A pitcher allows 4 earned runs over 18 innings. At that rate, how many earned runs would he allow over 27 innings?","visual":"table","answer":{"kind":"numeric","value":"6"},"misconceptionMap":{"5.33":"cross-multiplies-wrong-pairs"},"hints":["Keep runs on top, innings on the bottom: 4/18 = x/27.","Cross-multiply: 18·x = 4·27, so 18x = 108."],"difficulty":2},{"id":"ALG-E07-p1-baseball-03","version":1,"skillId":"ALG-E07","phase":1,"sport":"baseball","prompt":"A team scores 15 runs across 5 games. At that rate, how many runs would they score across a 12-game stretch?","visual":"table","answer":{"kind":"numeric","value":"36"},"misconceptionMap":{"4":"mismatched-units-in-ratio"},"hints":["runs/games = runs/games: 15/5 = x/12.","15/5 = 3 runs per game. Multiply by 12 games."],"difficulty":2},{"id":"ALG-E07-p1-baseball-04","version":1,"skillId":"ALG-E07","phase":1,"sport":"baseball","prompt":"A baserunner steals 14 bases in 35 attempts. Keeping that success rate, how many steals would he get in 50 attempts?","visual":"table","answer":{"kind":"numeric","value":"20"},"misconceptionMap":{"125":"mismatched-units-in-ratio"},"hints":["steals/attempts = steals/attempts: 14/35 = x/50.","14/35 = 0.4. Multiply by 50 attempts."],"difficulty":3},{"id":"ALG-E07-p1-softball-01","version":1,"skillId":"ALG-E07","phase":1,"sport":"softball","prompt":"A pitcher records 12 strikeouts over 8 innings. At that r